// Skills practice in the format of the Aptis General test, plus the "British corner".
window.DATA = window.DATA || {};

// READING
// type 'mcq': qs = [question, correct, wrong, wrong]   (or, with `fixed` options: [question, index of correct option])
// type 'order': s = sentences in the correct order (the first one is given to the learner)
DATA.reading = [
{ id: "r1", part: 1, type: "mcq", title: "An email to a friend", instr: "Choose the best word for each gap.",
  text: "Dear Sam,\nI'm writing to (1) ___ you about my new flat. It's quite small, but it's very (2) ___ to the station, so my journey to work only (3) ___ ten minutes. The neighbours are friendly and the (4) ___ is cheap. You must come and (5) ___ soon!\nLove, Jo",
  qs: [["Gap (1)","tell","say","speak"],["Gap (2)","close","along","beside"],["Gap (3)","takes","lasts","spends"],["Gap (4)","rent","wage","fare"],["Gap (5)","visit","arrive","reach"]] },
{ id: "r2", part: 2, type: "order", title: "Tea in Britain", s: [
  "Tea is now seen as Britain's national drink, but this was not always the case.",
  "It first arrived in the country from China in the 1650s.",
  "At that time, however, it was so expensive that only the rich could afford it.",
  "Because of these high prices, a lot of it was brought in illegally.",
  "This smuggling only stopped when the government cut the tax on it in 1784.",
  "After that, it quickly became the favourite drink of ordinary working people."] },
{ id: "r3", part: 3, type: "mcq", title: "Working from home", instr: "Four people give their opinions. Read the texts and answer the questions.",
  text: "PRIYA: I save two hours a day now that I don't commute, and I spend that time with my kids. I do miss chatting with colleagues, though. Video calls just aren't the same.\nTOM: Honestly, I get far less done at home. There's always something to distract me: the washing, the fridge, the dog. I went back to the office as soon as I could.\nMARTA: My company let us choose, and I go in twice a week. For me that's the perfect balance, although my flat is tiny, so I've had to turn a corner of the bedroom into an office.\nOWEN: I started my first job remotely and found it really hard to learn anything. Nobody could show me how things were done. Young employees need people around them.",
  fixed: ["Priya","Tom","Marta","Owen"],
  qs: [["Who finds it difficult to concentrate at home?",1],["Who thinks new workers lose out when they work remotely?",3],["Who has more family time than before?",0],["Who does not have enough space at home?",2],["Who feels online meetings are a poor substitute for real conversation?",0],["Who was given a choice by their employer?",2]] },
{ id: "r4", part: 4, type: "mcq", title: "The Mill Street garden", instr: "Choose the best heading for each paragraph. There are two headings you do not need.",
  text: "1. Ten years ago, the land behind Mill Street was an abandoned car park covered in broken glass. Today the same space produces tomatoes, beans and apples, thanks to a group of residents who simply got tired of looking at it.\n2. Nobody is in charge. Members meet on the first Sunday of each month to decide together what to plant and who will water it. New arrivals are given a small plot and paired with someone more experienced.\n3. Doctors at the local surgery have begun sending patients to the garden. They say that an hour of digging does as much for low mood and high blood pressure as some medicines, and it costs the health service nothing.\n4. The garden's future, however, is uncertain. The council owns the land and a developer has offered to buy it for housing. Members have collected four thousand signatures, but a final decision will not be made until spring.",
  fixed: ["A problem with the neighbours","From wasteland to green space","Making money from vegetables","How the project is run","Under threat","A prescription without pills"],
  qs: [["Paragraph 1",1],["Paragraph 2",3],["Paragraph 3",5],["Paragraph 4",4]] },
{ id: "r5", part: 1, type: "mcq", title: "A notice to staff", instr: "Choose the best word for each gap.",
  text: "NOTICE TO ALL STAFF\nThe car park will be (1) ___ from Monday while it is being resurfaced. Staff are (2) ___ to use public transport or the car park on Dean Road, which is a five-minute (3) ___ away. We apologise for any (4) ___ and expect the work to be finished (5) ___ Friday.",
  qs: [["Gap (1)","closed","ended","stopped"],["Gap (2)","advised","suggested","said"],["Gap (3)","walk","step","way"],["Gap (4)","inconvenience","disadvantage","complaint"],["Gap (5)","by","until","at"]] },
{ id: "r6", part: 2, type: "order", title: "A lucky mistake", s: [
  "Last winter, Dan booked a weekend in Dublin to surprise his girlfriend.",
  "When they got to the airport, though, he realised he had made a terrible mistake.",
  "The tickets he had bought were not for Dublin at all, but for Dubrovnik.",
  "Instead of going home, the couple decided to get on the plane anyway.",
  "They ended up loving the Croatian city so much that they stayed a whole week.",
  "Now they tell everyone it was the best holiday they have ever had."] },
{"id":"r7","part":1,"type":"mcq","title":"A message to a colleague","instr":"Choose the best word for each gap.","text":"Hi Ravi,\nJust a quick note to (1) ___ you that tomorrow's meeting has been moved to three o'clock. Could you (2) ___ the sales figures with you? I'm afraid I'll be a few minutes late because I have to (3) ___ my daughter up from school. If you have any problems, give me a (4) ___ on my mobile. Thanks for your (5) ___!\nHelen","qs":[["Gap (1)","remind","remember","recall"],["Gap (2)","bring","wear","lift"],["Gap (3)","pick","take","get"],["Gap (4)","ring","touch","hit"],["Gap (5)","help","favour","assist"]]},
{"id":"r8","part":1,"type":"mcq","title":"A postcard from Cornwall","instr":"Choose the best word for each gap.","text":"Dear Gran,\nWe're having a lovely time in Cornwall. The weather has been (1) ___ than we expected, so we've spent most days on the beach. Yesterday we (2) ___ on a boat trip to see the seals, and Jack was (3) ___ excited that he nearly fell in! The cottage is small but comfortable, and it's only a short walk (4) ___ the harbour. We'll be home on Sunday. I can't (5) ___ to show you the photos.\nLove, Emma","qs":[["Gap (1)","better","best","well"],["Gap (2)","went","took","made"],["Gap (3)","so","such","too"],["Gap (4)","from","of","at"],["Gap (5)","wait","hope","expect"]]},
{"id":"r9","part":1,"type":"mcq","title":"A hotel review","instr":"Choose the best word or phrase for each gap.","text":"We stayed at the Harbour View for three nights last month. (1) ___ the hotel is described as 'luxury' on its website, our room was small and rather dark. The staff, (2) ___, were friendly and did their best to help. Breakfast was excellent, but dinner was (3) ___ expensive for what we got. The location is perfect, (4) ___ you want to explore the old town on foot. On the whole, I would stay again, (5) ___ they gave me a room with a sea view.","qs":[["Gap (1)","Although","Despite","However"],["Gap (2)","however","although","despite"],["Gap (3)","far too","too much","enough"],["Gap (4)","especially if","even though","so that"],["Gap (5)","provided","unless","whereas"]]},
{"id":"r10","part":2,"type":"order","title":"A perfect cup of tea","s":["Making a good cup of tea is simple, but most people rush it.","First of all, fill the kettle with fresh water and switch it on.","While you are waiting for it to boil, put a tea bag into a mug.","As soon as the water is ready, pour it over the bag.","Then leave it for about four minutes so that the flavour can develop.","Finally, take the bag out and add a little milk if you like."]},
{"id":"r11","part":2,"type":"order","title":"The lost dog","s":["On Monday morning, Mrs Patel noticed that her dog, Biscuit, was not in the garden.","She searched the nearby streets for hours without finding him.","That evening, she decided to put up posters with his photo on them.","Two days later, a man phoned to say he had seen one of them outside the library.","He explained that a dog just like the one in the picture was sleeping in his garage.","Within an hour, Biscuit was back at home, eating his dinner."]},
{"id":"r12","part":2,"type":"order","title":"A new cycle path","s":["Last year the council asked residents what would improve the town centre.","Most of those who replied said that the roads were too dangerous for cyclists.","In response to this, plans were drawn up for a cycle path along the river.","However, the project was delayed when the cost turned out to be higher than expected.","The extra money was eventually found by cancelling a new car park.","As a result, building work finally began in March."]},
{"id":"r13","part":3,"type":"mcq","title":"Learning a language","instr":"Four people give their opinions. Read the texts and answer the questions.","text":"ELENA: I learnt English mostly from television series. I never opened a grammar book, and it shows: I speak quickly, but I still make basic mistakes when I write.\nJAMES: Apps are fine for vocabulary, but you can't have a real conversation with a phone. I only started improving when I joined an evening class and had to speak in front of other people.\nSOFIA: I spent a year in Dublin working in a café. It was exhausting at first, and I was too embarrassed to speak, but after three months I was dreaming in English.\nKARL: Honestly, I think some people simply have a talent for languages and others don't. I've studied French for ten years with every method there is, and I can still barely order a coffee.","fixed":["Elena","James","Sofia","Karl"],"qs":[["Who thinks natural ability matters more than the way you study?",3],["Who feels their writing is weaker than their speaking?",0],["Who learnt by living abroad?",2],["Who believes technology has limits for language learning?",1],["Who felt too shy to talk at the beginning?",2],["Who needed the pressure of a group to make progress?",1],["Who has had little success in spite of a lot of effort?",3]]},
{"id":"r14","part":3,"type":"mcq","title":"City or countryside?","instr":"Four people give their opinions. Read the texts and answer the questions.","text":"HANNAH: We moved out of London when the children were born. I don't miss the noise, but I do miss being able to walk to a cinema. Here you need the car for absolutely everything.\nOMAR: I grew up in a village and couldn't wait to leave. Everybody knew everybody's business. In the city nobody cares what you do, and I find that wonderfully relaxing.\nGRACE: People say the countryside is cheap. It isn't. Houses cost less, but the nearest supermarket is twenty miles away, and my heating bills are enormous.\nPETER: I work from home, so I can live wherever I like. I chose a small town by the sea: quieter than a city, but with a railway station and a decent hospital.","fixed":["Hannah","Omar","Grace","Peter"],"qs":[["Who enjoys the fact that city people leave you alone?",1],["Who thinks rural life costs more than people believe?",2],["Who depends on driving more than before?",0],["Who has found a compromise between the two ways of life?",3],["Who left the city for family reasons?",0],["Who was eager to move away from the place where they were brought up?",1],["Whose job makes it possible to choose where to live?",3]]},
{"id":"r15","part":3,"type":"mcq","title":"Should museums be free?","instr":"Four people give their opinions. Read the texts and answer the questions.","text":"NADIA: Free entry is one of the best things about Britain. I take my class every term, and half of those children would never go if their parents had to pay.\nTOM: I'd happily pay a few pounds. The big museums are so crowded with tourists taking selfies that you can't see the paintings. A small charge would thin the crowds.\nISABEL: Tourists like me already spend a fortune on hotels and restaurants here. Why not ask visitors from abroad to pay and keep it free for local people, as some countries do?\nDEREK: Somebody always pays. If it isn't the visitor, it's the taxpayer, including people in small towns who never get the chance to visit a London museum.","fixed":["Nadia","Tom","Isabel","Derek"],"qs":[["Who suggests different rules for residents and foreign visitors?",2],["Who points out that free museums are paid for by everyone?",3],["Who believes that charging would improve the visit?",1],["Who is thinking of children from poorer families?",0],["Who complains about how other visitors behave?",1],["Who mentions an unfairness between different parts of the country?",3]]},
{"id":"r16","part":4,"type":"mcq","title":"The four-day week","instr":"Choose the best heading for each paragraph. There are two headings you do not need.","text":"1. For most of the last century, a job meant five days in the office or the factory, and nobody questioned it. That pattern, however, is younger than many people think: until the 1930s, most people also worked on Saturday mornings.\n2. In 2022, around sixty British companies agreed to let their staff work four days for the same pay. The condition was simple: the same amount of work had to be done. Researchers followed the firms for six months.\n3. The results surprised even the organisers. Sales stayed the same or rose, fewer people were off sick, and far fewer employees resigned. Almost all the companies decided to continue after the trial ended.\n4. Not everyone is convinced. Hospitals, schools and shops cannot simply close on Fridays, and critics argue that squeezing five days of tasks into four leaves some workers more stressed, not less.\n5. What happens next depends largely on employers. No law is planned, so change will come company by company, as managers watch what their competitors do.","fixed":["Reasons for doubt","Advice for job hunters","Not as traditional as it seems","A slow road ahead","An experiment begins","The cost to the taxpayer","Better than expected"],"qs":[["Paragraph 1",2],["Paragraph 2",4],["Paragraph 3",6],["Paragraph 4",0],["Paragraph 5",3]]},
{"id":"r17","part":4,"type":"mcq","title":"Why we forget names","instr":"Choose the best heading for each paragraph. There are two headings you do not need.","text":"1. You are introduced to someone at a party, and ten seconds later their name has gone. It happens to almost everybody, and it has little to do with age or intelligence.\n2. Psychologists explain that a name is an unusually difficult thing to remember because it tells us nothing. The word 'baker' brings pictures to mind; the surname 'Baker' brings none.\n3. There is another reason. At the moment of meeting, we are busy thinking about what to say next and how we look, so the name never properly enters our memory in the first place.\n4. Fortunately, a few habits help. Repeat the name aloud straight away, use it once in the conversation, and connect it to something you can see, such as the person's glasses or jacket.\n5. And if it still disappears? Most people are far less offended than we fear. Admitting it with a smile is usually better than avoiding the person all evening.","fixed":["Simple tricks that work","How memory changes as we get older","A common experience","Honesty is the best policy","Words without meaning","Names that are going out of fashion","Too distracted to listen"],"qs":[["Paragraph 1",2],["Paragraph 2",4],["Paragraph 3",6],["Paragraph 4",0],["Paragraph 5",3]]},
{"id":"r18","part":4,"type":"mcq","title":"The return of the night train","instr":"Choose the best heading for each paragraph. There are two headings you do not need.","text":"1. Thirty years ago, sleeping carriages crossed Europe every night. Then cheap flights arrived, passenger numbers fell, and one service after another was closed.\n2. Now they are coming back. New routes link cities such as Brussels, Vienna and Berlin, and tickets for the summer often sell out months in advance.\n3. The main reason is the climate. A traveller who takes the train instead of the plane produces only a small part of the carbon, and more and more people say this affects their choice.\n4. There are practical advantages too. You leave from the city centre in the evening and arrive in another one at breakfast time, having saved the cost of a hotel room.\n5. The experience is not perfect, however. Carriages are often decades old, delays are frequent, and a private cabin can cost far more than a flight.","fixed":["Still some drawbacks","Safety on board","Growing demand","A way of travelling that almost disappeared","Jobs for railway workers","Time and money saved","A greener choice"],"qs":[["Paragraph 1",3],["Paragraph 2",2],["Paragraph 3",6],["Paragraph 4",5],["Paragraph 5",0]]}
];

// LISTENING — script lines are [speaker 'M' | 'F', text], read aloud with a British voice.
DATA.listening = [
{ id: "l1", part: 1, title: "A voicemail message", script: [["F","Hi Ben, it's Claire. I'm afraid I can't make lunch on Thursday. My boss has moved our team meeting to one o'clock. Could we do Friday instead? Same café, but let's say half past twelve rather than twelve, because I've got the dentist at eleven. Give me a ring this evening. I'll be home after seven."]],
  qs: [["Why can't Claire meet on Thursday?","She has a meeting at work","She is going to the dentist","She will be away"],["What time does she suggest for Friday?","12.30","12.00","1.00"],["When should Ben phone her?","After seven this evening","Before eleven","Tomorrow morning"]] },
{ id: "l2", part: 1, title: "A station announcement", script: [["M","This is a platform change for passengers travelling to Edinburgh. The ten forty-five service will now depart from platform nine, not platform four. We are sorry to announce that this train is running approximately twenty minutes late because of a signal failure near York. A trolley service of drinks and snacks will be available, but there is no restaurant carriage on this train."]],
  qs: [["Which platform will the train leave from?","Platform 9","Platform 4","Platform 5"],["Why is the train late?","There is a signal problem","The weather is bad","There are not enough staff"],["At about what time will the train leave?","11.05","10.45","11.20"],["What can passengers get on the train?","Drinks and snacks from a trolley","A meal in the restaurant carriage","Nothing at all"]] },
{ id: "l3", part: 3, title: "Looking for a flat", instr: "Who expresses each opinion: the man, the woman, or both?",
  script: [["F","So, what did you think of the flat on Park Road?"],["M","Well, it's got loads of space, hasn't it? Much bigger than ours."],["F","It is, I agree. But did you hear the traffic? I couldn't sleep with that."],["M","Really? It didn't bother me at all. I was more worried about the rent, to be honest."],["F","Yes, nine hundred a month is too much for us."],["M","And it's miles from the station."],["F","Oh, I don't mind a walk. It would do us good."]],
  fixed: ["The man","The woman","Both"],
  qs: [["The flat is spacious.",2],["The flat is too noisy.",1],["The rent is too high.",2],["It is too far from the station.",0]] },
{ id: "l4", part: 4, title: "Life without social media", script: [["F","I deleted all my social media apps in January. Everyone expected me to say that I suddenly had hours of free time and read fifty books. The truth is less exciting. For the first two weeks I kept picking up my phone and staring at it, with nothing to open. What I did notice, eventually, was that I stopped comparing my life with other people's holidays. I do miss seeing photos of my cousins in Australia, so I now ring them on Sundays instead, and, strangely, we talk more than we ever did."]],
  qs: [["What happened in the first two weeks?","She kept reaching for her phone out of habit","She felt relieved straight away","She read a lot of books"],["What does she see as the main benefit?","She no longer measures her life against other people's","She has much more free time","She saves money"],["What has happened to her relationship with her cousins?","They are actually in closer contact now","They have lost touch","They only write letters now"]] },
{ id: "l5", part: 2, title: "At the leisure centre", script: [["M","Good morning, Riverside Leisure Centre."],["F","Hello, I'd like to ask about swimming lessons for adults."],["M","Of course. We've got a beginners' class on Mondays at seven p.m. and an improvers' class on Wednesdays at half past six."],["F","I can swim a bit, but not very well, so the Wednesday one, I think. How much is it?"],["M","It's sixty pounds for ten weeks, or seven pounds fifty if you pay each week."],["F","I'll pay for the full course, then. Do I need to bring anything?"],["M","Just a swimming costume and a pound coin for the locker. You get the pound back afterwards."]],
  qs: [["Which class does the woman choose?","Wednesdays at 6.30","Mondays at 7.00","Wednesdays at 7.00"],["How much will she pay?","£60","£75","£7.50"],["Why does she need a pound coin?","To use a locker","To hire a towel","To pay for the car park"]] },
{ id: "l6", part: 4, title: "A street without cars", script: [["M","When the council first announced that the high street would be closed to cars, shop owners were furious. They were convinced that customers would simply drive to the retail park instead. Two years on, the figures tell a different story: the number of people visiting the street has risen by a third, and only one shop has closed, which, the owner admits, had been losing money for years anyway. That said, not everyone is happy. Residents of the neighbouring streets complain that the traffic has just moved to their front doors, and I think they have a point."]],
  qs: [["How did shop owners react at first?","They feared they would lose customers","They welcomed the idea","They were not interested"],["What do the figures show?","More people now visit the street","Many shops have closed","Drivers now go to the retail park"],["What is the speaker's attitude to the residents' complaints?","He thinks they are reasonable","He thinks they are exaggerating","He does not mention an opinion"]] },
{"id":"l7","part":1,"title":"A message about a delivery","script":[["M","Hello, this is a message for Mrs Carter from Green's Furniture. Your sofa was due to arrive on Tuesday, but I'm afraid the lorry has broken down, so we now expect to deliver it on Thursday between nine and midday. If nobody will be at home then, please ring the shop before five o'clock today and we'll arrange Saturday morning instead. We're very sorry for the inconvenience."]],"qs":[["Why is the delivery late?","The lorry has broken down","The sofa is not ready","The driver is ill"],["When will the sofa arrive now?","On Thursday morning","On Tuesday afternoon","On Saturday evening"],["What should Mrs Carter do if she cannot be at home?","Phone the shop before five today","Leave a key with a neighbour","Send an email"]]},
{"id":"l8","part":1,"title":"The weekend weather","script":[["F","And now the weather for the weekend. Saturday will start dry and bright across most of the country, but cloud will build during the afternoon, bringing heavy rain to Scotland by the evening. Sunday looks much colder, with temperatures falling to around four degrees, and there is a chance of snow on high ground in the north. In the south it should stay dry, although a strong wind will make it feel colder than it is."]],"qs":[["What will Saturday morning be like?","Dry and sunny","Wet and windy","Cold and snowy"],["Where will it rain on Saturday evening?","In Scotland","In the south","All over the country"],["How will Sunday compare with Saturday?","It will be much colder","It will be warmer","It will be about the same"],["What is the forecast for the south on Sunday?","Dry but windy","Snow showers","Warm and bright"]]},
{"id":"l9","part":1,"title":"A museum announcement","script":[["M","Good afternoon, and welcome to the City Museum. Please note that the museum will close at five o'clock today, an hour earlier than usual, because of a private event. The café on the ground floor will stop serving at half past four. The guided tour of the Roman gallery, which normally starts at two, will begin at a quarter past two this afternoon. Tickets for the tour cost three pounds and can be bought at the information desk."]],"qs":[["What time does the museum close today?","5.00","6.00","4.30"],["Why is it closing early?","There is a private event","There are building repairs","It is a public holiday"],["What time does the guided tour start today?","2.15","2.00","2.45"],["Where can visitors buy tickets for the tour?","At the information desk","In the café","In the Roman gallery"]]},
{"id":"l10","part":2,"title":"Booking a table","script":[["M","Good evening, The Olive Tree. How can I help?"],["F","Hello, I'd like to book a table for Saturday, please. There'll be six of us."],["M","Let me see. I'm afraid we're full at eight, but I could do half past six or a quarter past nine."],["F","Quarter past nine is a bit late for the children. We'll take the earlier one."],["M","Lovely. And is it a special occasion?"],["F","Yes, it's my father's seventieth birthday. Could we have a table by the window?"],["M","I can't promise that, but I'll make a note. Could I take a name?"],["F","It's Robinson."]],"qs":[["How many people is the table for?","Six","Seven","Eight"],["What time is the table booked for?","6.30","8.00","9.15"],["What are they celebrating?","A birthday","A wedding anniversary","A new job"],["What does the man say about a table by the window?","He will try, but he cannot guarantee it","It is already booked","It costs extra"]]},
{"id":"l11","part":2,"title":"At the ticket office","script":[["F","Next, please."],["M","Hi. I need to get to Bristol tomorrow morning. What's the cheapest way?"],["F","If you travel before nine, a return is eighty-four pounds. After half past nine, it drops to thirty-nine."],["M","That's quite a difference! My meeting isn't until two, so I'll go later."],["F","The ten fifteen gets in at twelve forty. Would that suit you?"],["M","Perfect. Do I need to reserve a seat?"],["F","It's not required, but I'd recommend it. It's free."],["M","Then yes, please. A window seat, if possible."]],"qs":[["How much will the man pay for his ticket?","£39","£84","£93"],["Why does he choose a later train?","It is much cheaper","He wants to sleep longer","The early train is full"],["What time does his train arrive in Bristol?","12.40","10.15","2.00"],["What does the woman say about reserving a seat?","It is free and a good idea","It is compulsory","It costs extra"]]},
{"id":"l12","part":2,"title":"Joining the library","script":[["M","Hello, I've just moved here and I'd like to join the library."],["F","Welcome! I'll need to see something with your address on it, like a bill."],["M","I've got my tenancy agreement on my phone. Is that all right?"],["F","That's fine. Now, you can borrow up to twelve books at a time, for three weeks."],["M","And what happens if I bring them back late?"],["F","There's a charge of twenty pence a day for each book, but children's books are free."],["M","Can I borrow films as well?"],["F","Not any more, I'm afraid, but you can use our computers for an hour a day without paying."]],"qs":[["What does the man show to prove his address?","A tenancy agreement","A gas bill","A passport"],["How long can he keep the books?","Three weeks","Twelve days","One month"],["What is true about the charge for late books?","It does not apply to children's books","It is twenty pounds a day","It applies only to films"],["What can members no longer borrow?","Films","Children's books","Computers"]]},
{"id":"l13","part":3,"title":"Planning a holiday","instr":"Who expresses each opinion: the man, the woman, or both?","script":[["F","I've been looking at holidays. What about a week in Greece?"],["M","Greece in August? It'll be boiling. I'd rather go somewhere cooler."],["F","Oh, I love the heat. That's the whole point of a holiday for me."],["M","Well, we both need a proper rest this year, that's for sure."],["F","Definitely. I'm exhausted. So no city breaks with museums all day."],["M","I quite like museums, actually. But I agree we shouldn't fly. The airports were a nightmare last year."],["F","Yes, let's take the train. That settles it, then: somewhere we can reach by rail."]],"fixed":["The man","The woman","Both"],"qs":[["Hot weather is an attraction.",1],["They need a relaxing holiday.",2],["Visiting museums is enjoyable.",0],["They should avoid flying.",2]]},
{"id":"l14","part":3,"title":"The new manager","instr":"Who expresses each opinion: the man, the woman, or both?","script":[["M","What do you think of Daniel, the new manager?"],["F","He seems very organised. The meetings actually finish on time now."],["M","True, I'll give him that. But I find him a bit cold. He never asks how you are."],["F","Really? He's always been friendly to me. He remembered my daughter's name."],["M","Hmm. Well, he certainly expects a lot. I've never worked so hard."],["F","Oh, absolutely. I've stayed late three times this week."],["M","Still, I suppose the department needed a change."],["F","I'm not so sure. Things were fine before."]],"fixed":["The man","The woman","Both"],"qs":[["Daniel is well organised.",2],["Daniel is unfriendly.",0],["Daniel is demanding.",2],["The department needed a change.",0]]},
{"id":"l15","part":3,"title":"Petrol or electric?","instr":"Who expresses each opinion: the man, the woman, or both?","script":[["F","So, have you decided? Petrol or electric?"],["M","Electric, I think. It's so much cheaper to run."],["F","It is, there's no doubt about that. But they cost a fortune to buy."],["M","The prices have come down a lot, you know. I don't think that's true any more."],["F","Well, what worries me is charging it. There's nowhere near our flat."],["M","That's my main worry too, to be honest."],["F","And I've read that the batteries don't last."],["M","I'm not convinced by that. My brother's is eight years old and it's fine."]],"fixed":["The man","The woman","Both"],"qs":[["Electric cars are cheap to run.",2],["Electric cars are too expensive to buy.",1],["Electric cars are more affordable than they used to be.",0],["Charging the car would be difficult.",2],["The batteries wear out quickly.",1]]},
{"id":"l16","part":4,"title":"Life without a car","script":[["F","Two years ago I sold my car. My friends assumed I'd done it to save the planet, and I let them think so, because it sounded better than the truth, which is that I simply couldn't afford the repairs. What I didn't expect was how little I'd miss it. I walk to work now, and I've lost count of the neighbours I've met on the way. The only time I regret it is when I visit my parents, who live in a village with two buses a day. On those weekends I borrow my sister's car and remember exactly why I used to love driving."]],"qs":[["Why did the speaker really sell her car?","She could not pay for the repairs","She wanted to protect the environment","She hardly ever used it"],["What unexpected benefit does she mention?","She has got to know people who live nearby","She has saved a great deal of money","She has become much fitter"],["How does she feel about driving now?","She still enjoys it from time to time","She has come to hate it","She is afraid of it"]]},
{"id":"l17","part":4,"title":"My first marathon","script":[["M","I ran my first marathon last spring, at the age of fifty-two. People keep telling me how brave that was, but honestly the race itself was the easy part: there were crowds cheering and a medal at the end. The hard part was the five months before, getting up in the dark in January to run alone in the rain. Twice I nearly gave up. What kept me going wasn't determination, I'm afraid. I had told everyone at work that I was doing it, and I couldn't face admitting that I'd stopped. Would I do it again? Ask me when my knees have recovered."]],"qs":[["What did the speaker find hardest?","The months of training","The race itself","Finding the time to run"],["Why didn't he give up?","He did not want to lose face with his colleagues","He is an unusually determined person","His family encouraged him"],["What is his attitude to running another marathon?","He avoids giving a clear answer","He has definitely decided to do it","He says he will never do it again"]]},
{"id":"l18","part":4,"title":"The village shop","script":[["F","When the last shop in our village closed, a group of us decided to reopen it ourselves. None of us had ever run a business, and it showed. In the first year we ordered far too much fresh food and threw half of it away. But we learned. Today the shop is staffed entirely by volunteers and just about covers its costs. It will never make anyone rich, and that was never the idea. For some of our older customers, the chat at the counter is the only conversation they have all day, and you can't put a price on that."]],"qs":[["What does the speaker say about the first year?","Their lack of experience led to waste","They made a large profit","Nobody was willing to help"],["What is the shop's financial situation now?","It more or less breaks even","It makes a healthy profit","It is losing a lot of money"],["What does she see as the shop's real value?","It gives lonely people some company","It sells cheaper food than the supermarkets","It creates paid jobs"]]}
];

